+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/TELND> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithology> "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/P"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/S597> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "[Obsolete: use TELND]"@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/TELND> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "The Teindland Palaeosol Formation is developed on the surface of the Orbliston Sand Bed and is podzolic in character. It comprises a thin redeposited humic 'H' horizon, a bleached 'Ea' horizon up to 15 cm thick, an intermittently developed iron pan and a lower 'strong brown' (7.5 YR 5/8) 'Bs' horizon, 5 to 15 cm thick. Overlying the humic horizon are thin layers of organic sand with charcoal fragments. At Teindland Quarry, the Badentinan Sand Bed, up to 1.5 m thick, overlies these organic sands. The lower 80-100 cm comprises parallel thin beds of brown polleniferous sand. This pollen was partly derived from reworking of soils around the site and partly from contemporaneous sparse grassland. The upper 50-70 cm of the sand is non-polleniferous. The presence of small gravel clusters, silt balls, an isoclinical fold and of shear zones suggest a glacial or glacitectonic influence on, or more likely following, deposition in a small pond. The Teindland Palaeosol Formation is less well developed at Red Burn, where the parent material is a greenish grey (5 GY 6/1) sandy diamicton and where the overlying organic sediments are thin and disturbed by cryoturbation."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/YOC> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLithologyDescription> "Coal."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/TELND> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasLowerBoundaryDefinition> "At Teindland Quarry, the Teindland Palaeosol Formation rests unconformably on up to 3 m of very pale brown (10 YR 7/4) sand (Orbliston Sand Bed of the Deanshillock Gravel Formation). At the Red Burn site, the Teindland Palaeosol Formation rests unconformably on a unit of sand with sporadic clasts that appears to correlate with the Deanshillock Gravel Formation at the base of the known sequence at Teindland Quarry."@en .
